extraction_notes: |
  - WB6 printed 145-146 (cache p146-p147), text layer read and both pages
    rendered to check every number. Alignment any, usually good or selfish.
    The class sits in the Rain Forest chapter under no group heading; it is a
    spell caster with no S.D.C. formula printed: occ_group magic,
    men_of_arms false.
  - XP: printed 169 says "Same as the Mystic O.C.C." The brief was to copy the
    production mystic''s xp_table, but the production `mystic` row (RUE) carries
    NO xp_table, so none is stored and the class uses the app''s default Rifts
    handling, exactly as the Mystic does. Revisit if the Mystic ever gets one.
  - Spell Magic (ability 4): six spells from levels 1-2 at first level
    (spells_starting 6, spell_levels_allowed [1, 2]); three from levels 1-3 at
    level 2; three from levels 1-4 at level 3; then "each additional level
    after the fourth" one spell up to the shaman''s own level. Stored
    literally: no spell at level 4, one per level at 5-15 capped at the
    character''s level. This may be a misprint for "from the fourth" (the RUE
    Mystic''s wording is "at fourth and each level after"); if so, add a level-4
    entry. The book names no spell and no tradition (it points to the Rifts RPG
    spell list), so general invocations only and no spell_traditions_allowed.
  - Exorcism and Turn Dead are the class''s own rites with their own
    percentages (8%/level; 20% +5%/level), stored as special_abilities, not
    granted as the catalog spells of the same names. Same call as the Voodoo
    Priest draft.
  - P.P.E. 2D4x10 plus P.E., +2D6 per additional level.
  - Bonuses: +1 to save vs poisons, drugs and disease stored as
    toxins_poisons 1, harmful_drugs 1, disease 1; +2 horror_factor.
  - Skills: base = catalog base + printed bonus. Native tongue and Creole at
    98%: Native Tongue 98 plus one Language: Other fixed at 98 (the Animal
    Shaman''s English precedent). Monster/Demon lore -> Lore: Demons & Monsters,
    Track Animal -> Track & Trap Animals. "Pilot: Boat and Sail (+15%)" stored
    as Boat: Sail Type 60+15 - a judgement; it could be read as a boat skill
    plus a sail skill, but the catalog''s sail row is the one that fits the
    printed name. W.P. Spear carries the book''s "(limited targeting)" as a
    note; its meaning is not explained on the page.
  - Hand to Hand: Expert costs one "other" skill, Martial Arts two; no
    Assassin offered.
  - Related: six, plus two at level 3 and one each at 6, 9 and 12.
    Pilot "horsemanship or exotic animal and boats only" is split between the
    Horsemanship category (General, Exotic Animals) and Pilot (the four
    surface Boat rows; Boat: Submersibles is left out as not a boat in a
    tribal sense - a judgement). Technical "art, language and lore only" is an
    only-list of Art, Language: Other, Language: Spanish and every Rifts Lore
    row in the catalog, so a lore row added later will not appear until it is
    added here.
  - Secondary: the book gives two at each of levels 2, 5, 8 and 12 and none
    at level one, read literally: count 0 with that schedule. If the intended
    reading is two at creation plus more later, count becomes 2.
  - Equipment: the weapon "of choice" is a choice from the WB6 biomancy,
    Manoan techno-wizard and Colombian energy weapons already in the catalog -
    a curated set, not exhaustive. Small bag for possessions plus "another
    small bag or two" for herbs stored as two small sacks (the book allows a
    third). Hunting/fishing spear -> short-spear. Knife or machete is a choice.
    Sparse clothing -> clothing. Biomancer or light M.D.C. armor is a choice of
    the two light biomancer armors and two light M.D.C. suits.
  - Money: 2D6x100 credits, all of it in trade items; stored as the coin
    figure.
---

## Lore

The tribal shamans of the South American rain forest are part priest, part magician. They are animists: every living thing, and many things that are not alive, has a spirit they can reach through ritual - speaking with plant and animal spirits, controlling and banishing entities, and casting spells.

A shaman earns his knowledge through ordeals of fasting, meditation, hallucinogenic plants and physical hardship. Each time he reaches a new level of experience he must undergo another ordeal of at least a day, and during it spirits or visions may come to him with information or insight.

Since the Great Cataclysm tribal shamans have grown more influential than ever; most rain forest tribes are led by a shaman or keep one as second in command or trusted advisor. They make up about a quarter of the magic practitioners among the tribes. Biomancers, mystics and warlocks may fill the role of a tribe''s "shaman" too, but without any of this O.C.C.''s abilities or bonuses.

## GM Notes

The G.M. usually chooses the kind of ordeal each new level demands; the book asks that it be fair and reasonable. The shaman''s armor is worn only when adventuring or expecting combat.
